History of finalg and (recent) use of AI

The 'finalg' is a reorganized version of my 'abstract_algebra' repository, which has now be archived. The primary change I made to 'abstract_algebra', that resulted in 'finalg', was to break out the single, monolithic source file, 'finite_algebras.py', into many smaller files: group.py, ring.py, etc. There were other changes that I can no longer recall. I'm a mathematician, not a programmer, and I found it daunting to make so many changes in-place, so I did all of it in a new repo, 'finalg'. By the way, I'm also not an algebraist. My area is mathematical statistics, but I'm facinated by algebra.

More recently, I've experimented with using AI to assist me with writing code. That's not something I want to do a lot of, but there were a couple of areas that vexed me, associativity and isomorphisms. I used brute-force algorithms for both and they were slow. So, I used Claude.AI to do the following:

* Rewrite my methods: 'Magma.fast_isomorphic', 'CayleyTable.is_associative', & 'Group.solvable'
* Sped up my implementation of 'about_subalgebras', including the 'binary_operation' class.
* Use my Jupyter notebook examples in 'abstract_algebra/notebooks' to write many new unittests
* Write two interoperability methods for converting my algebras to/from SymPy algebras
* Help organizing the structure of the 'finalg' repo
* Rewrite my 'README.md' for the 'finalg' repo

Details of some of my interactions with Claude.AI can be found in 'CHANGELOG.md' and in the Jupyter notebooks (.ipynb) in 'scratch/'.
